⟦P0⟧Tutorial: Developing a RESTful ⟦P1⟧ with Go and Gin⟦P2⟧This tutorial introduces the basics of writing a RESTful web service ⟦P3⟧ with Go⟦P4⟧and the ⟦P5⟧Gin Web Framework⟦P6⟧ (Gin).⟦P7⟧You'll get the most out of this tutorial if you have a basic familiarity with Go⟦P8⟧and its tooling. If this is your first exposure to Go, please see⟦P9⟧⟦P10⟧Tutorial: Get started with Go⟦P11⟧⟦P12⟧for a quick introduction.⟦P13⟧Gin simplifies many coding tasks associated with building web applications,⟦P14⟧including web services. In this tutorial, you'll use Gin to route requests,⟦P15⟧retrieve request details, and marshal ⟦P16⟧ for responses.⟦P17⟧In this tutorial, you will build a RESTful ⟦P18⟧ server with two endpoints. Your⟦P19⟧example project will be a repository of data about vintage jazz records.⟦P20⟧The tutorial includes the following sections:⟦P21⟧Design ⟦P22⟧ endpoints.⟦P23⟧Create a folder for your code.⟦P24⟧Create the data.⟦P25⟧Write a handler to return all items.⟦P26⟧Write a handler to add a new item.⟦P27⟧Write a handler to return a specific item.⟦P28⟧⟦P29⟧Note:⟦P30⟧ For other tutorials, see ⟦P31⟧Tutorials⟦P32⟧.⟦P33⟧To try this as an interactive tutorial you complete in Google Cloud Shell,⟦P34⟧click the button below.⟦P35⟧⟦P36⟧⟦P37⟧Open in Cloud Shell⟦P38⟧⟦P39⟧⟦P40⟧Prerequisites⟦P41⟧⟦P42⟧Go.⟦P43⟧ We recommend using the latest version of Go to follow this tutorial.⟦P44⟧For installation instructions, see ⟦P45⟧Installing Go⟦P46⟧.⟦P47⟧⟦P48⟧A tool to edit your code.⟦P49⟧ Any text editor you have will work fine.⟦P50⟧⟦P51⟧A command terminal.⟦P52⟧ Go works well using any terminal on Linux and Mac,⟦P53⟧and on ⟦P54⟧ or cmd in Windows.⟦P55⟧⟦P56⟧The curl tool.⟦P57⟧ On Linux and Mac, this should already be installed. On⟦P58⟧Windows, it's included on Windows 10 Insider build 17063 and later. For earlier⟦P59⟧Windows versions, you might need to install it. For more, see⟦P60⟧⟦P61⟧Tar and Curl Come to Windows⟦P62⟧.⟦P63⟧Design ⟦P64⟧ endpoints ⟦P65⟧⟦P66⟧You'll build an ⟦P67⟧ that provides access to a store selling vintage recordings⟦P68⟧on vinyl. So you'll need to provide endpoints through which a client can get⟦P69⟧and add albums for users.⟦P70⟧When developing an ⟦P71⟧, you typically begin by designing the endpoints. Your⟦P72⟧API's users will have more success if the endpoints are easy to understand.⟦P73⟧Here are the endpoints you'll create in this tutorial.⟦P74⟧⟦P75⟧ – Get a list of all albums, returned as ⟦P76⟧.⟦P77⟧⟦P78⟧ – Add a new album from request data sent as ⟦P79⟧.⟦P80⟧⟦P81⟧ – Get an album by its ⟦P82⟧, returning the album data as ⟦P83⟧.⟦P84⟧Next, you'll create a folder for your code.⟦P85⟧Create a folder for your code ⟦P86⟧⟦P87⟧To begin, create a project for the code you'll write.⟦P88⟧Open a command prompt and change to your home directory.⟦P89⟧On Linux or Mac:⟦P90⟧On Windows:⟦P91⟧Using the command prompt, create a directory for your code called⟦P92⟧web-service-gin.⟦P93⟧Create a module in which you can manage dependencies.⟦P94⟧Run the ⟦P95⟧ command, giving it the path of the module your code⟦P96⟧will be in.⟦P97⟧This command creates a ⟦P98⟧ file in which dependencies you add will be⟦P99⟧listed for tracking. For more about naming a module with a module path, see⟦P100⟧⟦P101⟧Managing dependencies⟦P102⟧.⟦P103⟧Next, you'll design data structures for handling data.⟦P104⟧Create the data ⟦P105⟧⟦P106⟧To keep things simple for the tutorial, you'll store data in memory. A more⟦P107⟧typical ⟦P108⟧ would interact with a database.⟦P109⟧Note that storing data in memory means that the set of albums will be lost each⟦P110⟧time you stop the server, then recreated when you start it.⟦P111⟧Write the code⟦P112⟧Using your text editor, create a file called ⟦P113⟧ in the web-service⟦P114⟧directory. You'll write your Go code in this file.⟦P115⟧Into ⟦P116⟧, at the top of the file, paste the following package declaration.⟦P117⟧A standalone program (as opposed to a library) is always in package ⟦P118⟧.⟦P119⟧Beneath the package declaration, paste the following declaration of